Usage: strings [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Print printable strings from FILE(s).

For each FILE, write to standard output all printable character sequences
that are at least MIN characters long. With no FILE, read standard input.

Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.

  -a, --all                scan each file in its entirety (default)
  -n, --bytes=MIN          print sequences of at least MIN characters (default 4)
  -t, --radix=RADIX        print offset before each string: o=octal, d=decimal, x=hex
  -e, --encoding=ENCODING  select character encoding:
                             s=7-bit-ascii (default), S=8-bit-UTF8,
                             b=16-bit-big-endian, l=16-bit-little-endian,
                             B=32-bit-big-endian, L=32-bit-little-endian
  -o                       print offset before each string (alias for -t o)

Exit status:
  0  if any string was found
  1  if no string was found or error occurred

OPTIONS:
  -a, --all                     scan each file in its entirety (default)
  -d, --data                    scan only object data sections
                                (placeholder/no-op: BFD section parsing is not
                                linked in this Windows build)
  -f, --print-file-name         print the file name before each string
  -n, --bytes ARG               print sequences of at least MIN printable
                                characters (default 4)
  -NUM                          same as --bytes=MIN
  -t, --radix ARG               print the offset within the file before each
                                string
  -w, --include-all-whitespace  include all whitespace as valid string
                                characters
  -e, --encoding ARG            select character encoding: s=7-bit-ascii,
                                S=8-bit-UTF8, b=16-bit-big-endian,
                                l=16-bit-little-endian, B=32-bit-big-endian,
                                L=32-bit-little-endian
  -o                            print offset before each string (alias for -t o)
  -s, --output-separator ARG    string used to separate parsed strings in output
  -T, --target ARG              specify binary file format (placeholder/no-op:
                                BFD target selection is not linked in this
                                Windows build)
  -U, --unicode ARG             unicode display mode (placeholder/no-op except
                                non-default modes select UTF-8 scanning)
      --help                    display this help and exit
  -V, --version                 output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
